.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt GRUB-BTRFS-RESTORE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm grub-btrfs-restore
.Nd make a snapper snapshot the root filesystem again \" codespell:ignore
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar number
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
replaces the root subvolume with a writable copy of snapper snapshot
.Ar number .
It works from the running system and from a snapshot booted through the
.Dq Debian snapshots
GRUB submenu.
Nothing is deleted: the previous root is renamed to
.Pa <subvol>.old-<date>
and all snapshots are carried over.
The change takes effect at the next boot.
.Pp
The root subvolume is the one named by the
.Cm subvol=
option for
.Pa /
in
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Pa @rootfs
on a default Debian install.
Other subvolumes such as
.Pa @home
are never touched.
.Sh HOW THE PACKAGE IS WIRED
.Bl -bullet
.It
On installation, if
.Pa /
is btrfs and no snapper config named
.Dq root
exists, one is created with timeline snapshots off and at most 10
numbered snapshots.
An existing config is never modified; tune it with
.Xr snapper 8 .
.It
That config arms snapper's own apt hook
.Pa /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/80snapper :
every apt transaction is wrapped in a pre/post snapshot pair.
Skip one with
.Dl DISABLE_APT_SNAPSHOT=yes apt install ...
.It
.Xr grub-btrfsd 8
watches
.Pa /.snapshots
and regenerates the GRUB submenu described in
.Xr grub-btrfs 8 .
.It
Removing or purging the package keeps the snapper config and every
snapshot.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
An upgrade broke the system.
Pick the
.Dq pre
snapshot of that transaction, restore it, reboot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
snapper list
grub-btrfs-restore 42
reboot
.Ed
.Pp
If the system no longer boots, choose the snapshot in the GRUB submenu
first and run the same commands there.
.Pp
Once satisfied, delete the previous root.
Its name is printed by
.Nm :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -o subvolid=5 "$(findmnt -nvo SOURCE /)" /mnt
btrfs subvolume delete /mnt/@rootfs.old-20261110-120000
umount /mnt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr btrfs-subvolume 8 ,
.Xr grub-btrfs 8 ,
.Xr grub-btrfsd 8 ,
.Xr snapper 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Kernels on a separate
.Pa /boot
partition are not part of any snapshot.
A snapshot taken before a kernel was purged has no matching
.Pa /lib/modules
and will not boot; after restoring across a kernel upgrade run
.Dl apt install --reinstall linux-image-amd64
so that
.Pa /boot
and the package database agree again.
.Pp
Only the layout made by
.Dq snapper create-config /
is supported, with
.Pa /.snapshots
nested in the root subvolume.
